# 00 — Editor (host GUI over libarbc)
#
# The Arbitrary Composer Editor: a native C++ desktop application that uses
# libarbc (v0.1+) as a still-image compositor where cells are placed by
# arbitrary affine and observed by cameras. Design is fully specified in
# docs/00-design.md (D1-D19) and docs/01-architecture.md (A1-A7); each leaf note
# cites the governing decision. Refinements land under tasks/refinements/.
#
# libarbc is an EXTERNAL dependency, consumed via CMake FetchContent from a git
# ref (github.com/ruoso/arbitrarycomposer) — it is not part of this WBS. A leaf
# that needs a library capability the released surface does not yet expose files
# that gap as a task in the LIBRARY repo, releases it, and consumes it here.
#
# Build order flows roughly top-to-bottom: foundation -> dockspace -> project &
# app state -> canvas -> cameras/cells -> panels/paint/import -> packaging.
#
# DEFINITION OF DONE (every leaf — docs/01-architecture.md §9, A8/A9 — NOT
# standalone leaves): respects the levelization DAG (check_levels clean); lands
# its tests (L1 logic -> Catch2 unit; renders -> render_offline golden; has UI ->
# an ImGui Test Engine e2e; threads -> ASan/TSan-clean); clang-format + build
# clean; scripts/gate green. foundation.build stands up the harness, the
# check_levels lint, the component skeleton, and scripts/gate so this bar is
# satisfiable from the first task. Each refinement's Acceptance-criteria section
# names the SPECIFIC tests that instantiate the DoD for its leaf.

task editor "Editor — host GUI over libarbc" {
  note "Every leaf carries the universal DEFINITION OF DONE (see file header / docs/01-architecture.md §9): levelization (check_levels) + layered tests (Catch2 L1 unit, render_offline goldens, ImGui Test Engine e2e for UI, ASan/TSan for threads) + format/build, all via scripts/gate. These are acceptance criteria on every task, not separate leaves."

  # ---- Foundation & scaffold (A1/A2/A3) ----
  task foundation "Foundation & scaffold" {
    task build "CMake, deps, presets, CI" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      note "Bootstrapped BY HAND (the loop cannot produce its own gate). CMakeLists.txt FetchContents libarbc as a subproject — arbc::arbc linked, binding proven by a Catch2 test — plus presets (dev/release/asan/tsan/coverage); the src/ 12-component skeleton (§7/§8 levels); scripts/check_levels.py enforcing the level DAG AND the A8 seam (the L1 core carries no ImGui/GL/SDL/libarbc leak); the Catch2 L1 test harness; scripts/gate (check_levels · clang-format · build · ctest); and the GitHub CI (ci.yml lint + gcc/clang × debug/release/asan/tsan + coverage) with the act runner for the orchestrator's in-loop replay. All green. SDL3 + Dear ImGui (docking) + the window/dockspace + the ImGui Test Engine harness land with app_shell. Arch A1/A2/A8/A9. Design: docs/01-architecture.md §7-§9. Refinement: tasks/refinements/foundation_build.md"
    }
    task app_shell "SDL3 window + GL context + ImGui + main loop" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !build
      note "FetchContent SDL3 + Dear ImGui (docking branch). Open a window, create an OpenGL context targeting the GLES3/WebGL2-common subset, init Dear ImGui with the SDL3+GL backends, run the frame loop. Self-rendered UI only (no native widgets) so the WASM target stays reachable. Being the FIRST ImGui surface, also stand up the ImGui Test Engine e2e harness + an offscreen-GL headless smoke — the DoD's UI-test rig every later view leaf uses. Arch A2/A3/A9. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/app_shell.md"
    }
    task platform_services "PlatformServices seam (file / threads / clock)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !app_shell
      note "A thin interface over directory/file access, thread spawn, and clock; native implementation (std filesystem + threads). The one seam the future web port swaps (File System Access / OPFS / Emscripten pthreads) without touching app or UI code. Arch A3. Refinement: tasks/refinements/foundation/platform_services.md"
    }
    task render_probe "Render a trivial Document into a canvas texture" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !app_shell
      note "Build a trivial libarbc Document in-process, render it into a CPU surface, upload as a GL texture, display it in one ImGui pane. Proves the binding + display path end to end and surfaces any library gap early. Arch A1/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/foundation/render_probe.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Dockspace shell (D18) ----
  task dock "Dockspace shell" {
    task dockspace "Split-tree + tabs over ImGui docking" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.foundation.app_shell
      note "A fully-uniform dockspace on ImGui's docking branch: recursive split containers, tabbed views, drag-to-dock, resize. No privileged editor area, no keep-a-canvas guardrail. Design: docs/00-design.md D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/dockspace.md"
    }
    task view_registry "View abstraction: open / close / reopen" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !dockspace
      note "Register view types (Canvas, Layers, Inspector, Overview, Color, History, Assets, Export) and instantiate them into any container; a view can be closed and reopened. Underpins 'anything can be closed'. Design: D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/view_registry.md"
    }
    task tool_rail "Fixed tool rail + view launcher (home base)" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !view_registry
      note "The thin left rail: modal tools (select/brush/eyedropper/camera/import/pan) plus the view launcher that reopens any closed view. Since anything (even every canvas) can be closed, the rail is the home base that can never leave you empty. Design: D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/tool_rail.md"
    }
    task workspaces "Saved layout presets (local UI state)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !dockspace, editor.foundation.platform_services
      note "Named workspace presets (Paint/Compose/Review) switchable in a click; the layout tree persists as LOCAL UI state in workspace/ or global prefs, never in the portable project.arbc. Design: D18 / §9. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.dock/workspaces.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Project & app state (A4/A7, D16/D19) ----
  task project "Project & app state" {
    task open "Open a project directory; new project" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.foundation.render_probe
      note "Open a project DIRECTORY (project.arbc + assets/ + workspace/): map the workspace or load the canonical doc; create a new project scaffolding the directory. Design: D16. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/open.md"
    }
    task open_ui "In-app New/Open affordance; recent projects; native folder picker" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !exec_new, editor.dock.tool_rail
      note "File-menu / tool-rail New and Open entries, a recent-projects list persisted to per-user prefs, and a native folder picker (SDL_ShowOpenFolderDialog behind a PlatformServices dialog seam), wiring the chosen path to ace::project::open_project / create_project and, for open-another, to editor.project.exec_new (new exec, A7/D19). Design: docs/00-design.md D16, D19. Source-of-debt refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/open.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/open_ui.md"
    }
    task app_state "Project-level state; single Document per process; command dispatch" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !open
      note "The app owns exactly ONE libarbc Document for its lifetime (process-per-project). Project-level selection and active tool; a command/action dispatch that turns UI actions into library transactions. Design: D19 / arch A4/A7. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/app_state.md"
    }
    task exec_new "Open-another-project = exec a new instance" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !app_state
      note "Opening a different project execs a fresh editor process (its own Document/workspace/threads/window). No multi-project-in-one-window, no in-process switching. Design: D19. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/exec_new.md"
    }
    task save "Save = re-dump project.arbc + assets; dirty state" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !app_state
      note "Save re-emits the canonical project.arbc (+ changed owned tiles) from the live workspace — a publish step, not a crash race (the workspace is durable by default). Dirty indicator = workspace-vs-snapshot drift. Design: D16 §9. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/save.md"
    }
    task save_as "Save As = publish + copy project dir + exec sibling" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !save, !exec_new
      note "Save As: dump the current state, recursively copy project.arbc + assets/ to the chosen target (excluding workspace/, which the sibling regenerates), write the workspace/-excluding .gitignore, then hand the new directory to commands::open_another_project (editor.project.exec_new). Design: D16 §9 (Save As = copy the directory), A7/D19 (process-per-project). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/save.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/save_as.md"
    }
    task undo "Undo/redo wired to library transactions" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !app_state
      note "Undo/redo IS libarbc's transaction journal (doc 14), not a reimplementation; continuous gestures (a stroke, a drag) coalesce into one step. Viewport-camera navigation is transient (not undoable); a saved shot's framing is scene data (undoable). Design: D15. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/undo.md"
    }
    task gc "Clean up (GC)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !save
      note "Clean up runs the library's gc_project_directory over assets/ (roots = this one document); GC never touches workspace/ or borrowed files. Consolidate + relink split to editor.import.consolidate. Design: D13. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/gc.md"
    }
    task welcome "Welcome dialog: interactive no-arg launch offers New / Open / Recent" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !open_ui
      note "Interactive no-arg launch (empty project_dir, non-headless) shows a modal welcome offering New / Open / Open Recent, backed by the dock::ProjectGateway (A12) the tool rail already uses. The launcher process owns NO Document: New/Open/Recent spawn a detached sibling on the chosen (or newly created) project directory (commands::open_another_project, spawn-not-swap, A7/D19) and the launcher then exits. Headless no-arg keeps auto-scratch (shell.cpp scratch_project_dir) so the driven path always holds a Document. Refines A7 (a pre-project launcher owns zero Documents; a project process still owns exactly one for its lifetime) and SUPERSEDES D-app_state-6 (scratch-when-none-given as the interactive default). Design: D16/D19/D22, arch A7/A12. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/app_state.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/welcome.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): dismissing/cancelling the welcome without choosing = the launcher EXITS cleanly (nothing to fall back to; keeps the zero-Document invariant absolute — a user wanting a throwaway chooses New). THREE verbs only (New / Open / Open Recent), matching D22 — no scratch/skip verb and no remembered-default in v1."
    }
    task entry_outcome "Distinguish refused-target from failed-spawn at the ProjectGateway seam" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !welcome
      note "Replace the entry verbs' single bool return with a small dock-local outcome POD (e.g. ProjectEntryOutcome: succeeded / refused_target / spawn_failed) so dock::WelcomeWindow and dock::Dockspace can surface 'That folder is not a project.' vs 'Could not start the editor.' without inferring spawn failure from MRU state. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/welcome.md (D-welcome deviation: Constraint 6 inference). Design: D22, arch A12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/entry_outcome.md"
    }
    task save_as_outcome "Widen save_as to the outcome seam so a failed publish or exec stops reporting a name refusal" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !entry_outcome
      note "AppProjectGateway::save_as (project_gateway.cpp:194-218) has four documented failure modes (dock.hpp:128-130: invalid name · target exists · failed publish · failed exec) collapsed into one bool; the entry_outcome leaf's adapter maps all four onto refused_target, preserving today's exact string. Resolving honestly needs a fourth enumerator (publish_failed) and a richer result from commands::save_project_as, which is L1 work outside the entry-verbs scope. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/entry_outcome.md. Design: D22/D27, arch A12/A13. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/save_as_outcome.md"
    }
    task save_as_rollback "Remove a partially published Save As target when the publish fails" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !save_as_outcome
      note "project::save_project_as creates the target tree before it can fail (src/project/save.cpp:195-239): a SaveError from the publish (:230) or from the gitignore write (:237) leaves a directory behind, and D27's guard (:205) then refuses the same name forever. save_as_outcome makes the message honest ('Could not save a copy there.'); making the state recoverable needs a recursive-remove faculty on platform::FileSystem (native impl + test double + A3 WASM seam), which is a new L0 method outside the 0.5d reporting change. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/save_as_outcome.md. Design: D27, arch A3/A12/A13. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.project/save_as_rollback.md"
    }
    task create_rollback "Remove a partial project scaffold when create_project fails after its existence guard" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      depends !save_as_rollback
      note "project::create_project (src/project/project_open.cpp:301-352) has the identical shape as save_project_as: it refuses an existing target at :312-314, then scaffolds with four early returns (:321 mkdir loop, :327 gitignore write, :335 workspace-document mint, :339 checkpoint), each leaving a partial directory that D27's guard refuses forever. Deferred from save_as_rollback because two of the four failure branches occur after an mmap-backed arbc::Document has been minted over workspace/ (:332-340), so the rollback must destroy the Document before removing the tree — a destruction-ordering constraint the pure-write Save As path does not have. The primitive (platform::FileSystem::remove_tree), its native impl, and all six doubles shipped with save_as_rollback; this leaf adds the two call sites plus tests in tests/project_open_test.cpp. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.project/save_as_rollback.md. Design: D27, arch A3."
    }
    task dir_is_project "Project = directory: New and Save As refuse any existing target" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !open, !save_as
      note "The project directory IS the project (D16, project-as-a-directory): creation targets a path that does not yet exist, and the tool creates the directory. Tighten project::create_project (project_open.cpp) and project::save_project_as (save.cpp) to refuse ANY existing target directory — not just one already holding a project.arbc — so New and Save As behave identically; surface the refusal as a clear in-app message via dock::ProjectGateway. REVERSES D-save_as-4's narrow 'a populated directory without project.arbc is a fine destination' guard (flips the save_as_test populated-target case) and adds a create-time existence check to the open path. Design: D16 (project-as-a-directory), D19. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/save_as.md, tasks/refinements/editor/open.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/dir_is_project.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): New AND Save As collect the target via a parent-location pick + typed project NAME (not a folder picker returning an existing dir); the tool creates that directory. 'Existing' means exists-at-all — refuse even an EMPTY existing dir, for full New/Save-As symmetry. So Save As switches from the shipped native-folder-pick UX to the same parent+name compose flow New uses."
    }
    task raster_tile_store "Thread RasterTileStore/TileDecodeDispatch through save/open for raster memoisation" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.model
      note "Thread arbc::RasterTileStore (and TileDecodeDispatch) through project::save_project -> arbc::builtin_codecs(registry, &tiles) (document_serialize.hpp:134) and project::open_project -> arbc::load_document(..., tiles, decode) (:218) so raster-cell tiles are memoized across saves instead of re-hashed on every save. save.cpp:120 passes null tiles and project_open.cpp:97-98 passes none -- correct but O(all tiles) per save. editor.cells.model is the first leaf to put raster cells in a saved project, making this load-bearing. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/model.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/raster_tile_store.md"
    }
    task reopen_degradation_notice "Surface unbindable-content count as a one-shot open notice" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cameras.reopen_slab_adopt
      note "Read OpenedProject::unbindable_content_records through the existing dock::ProjectGateway POD seam and show a one-shot notice on open naming how many objects the unsaved workspace could not recover, with an ImGui Test Engine e2e driving it headless by widget id. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/reopen_slab_adopt.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A19. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/reopen_degradation_notice.md"
    }
    task reconstructing_reopen "Restore the durable-by-default workspace fast path via the reconstructing reopen" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !reopen_degradation_notice
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#19 shipped in v0.4.0 and answers A19's 'Future fix (cross-repo)' clause in BOTH the shapes it named. (a) A registry-aware open: `arbc::open_document(path, registry, codecs, bridge, ...)` (arbc/runtime/document_serialize.hpp:190) rebuilds each recovered content through the same routing `load_document` uses for a canonical file, because `ContentRecord` now carries the content's construction identity (reverse-DNS kind_id + the kind's canonical params) and its input edges, captured at `add_content` through the kind's own registered codec via `Document::set_content_identity_capture` (`arbc::codec_identity_capture(codecs, bridge)` is the ready-made one). (b) A public rebind seam: `Document::rebind_content(id, content)` binds an object to a record that already exists — the repair route `add_content` could not serve, since it mints a NEW id while the recovered layers, journal edges and state handles all name the old one. `Document::recovered_content_state()` ships with it, finally making the arbc#5 replay trio reachable from a host that opens through `Document` rather than `Model`. SCOPE. Install the identity capture on the create/open path so newly written workspaces carry construction identity; open through `open_document` in project::open_project (src/project/project_open.cpp:245) and RETIRE the content-bearing-map guard A19 consequence (2) describes — the map fast path becomes durable-by-default again, for every kind at once, which is D-open-3's original policy. A record that cannot be rebuilt (a plugin absent this session, a kind with no codec, a workspace written before v0.4.0) arrives in `ReopenedDocument::unreconstructed` and is left UNBOUND — never filled with a default-constructed stand-in — so it feeds the existing `OpenedProject::unbindable_content_records` count and editor.project.reopen_degradation_notice keeps working unchanged, now reporting a genuinely rare case instead of the common one. AMEND A19 in docs/01-architecture.md: its premise ('the map path binds no Content for any kind') no longer holds at the pinned version and its consequences (1)-(3) are superseded; retain the text as the historical record the way A15 was retained, and keep the never-saved-project residual honest — a created-but-never-saved project still has no canonical floor, but its map now reconstructs, which is the whole point. CARRIES THE DIRTY-PRECISION DECISION (promoted from tasks/parking-lot.md 'Cross-session dirty precision on mapped-workspace reopen', D-save-4, at the 2026-07-28 triage): restoring the fast path FLIPS the common reopen from clean to dirty. Today most reopens rebuild from canonical and start clean because that read proves sync; after this leaf most reopens map, and the session-scoped dirty model starts them dirty, so a project saved cleanly yesterday opens showing modified with Save armed and a pointless re-dump on first press. That is a visible regression this leaf introduces, so this leaf owns the answer — and the parked entry's own reason for deferring the fix ('would require touching the shipped open/create path') is void here, because this leaf touches exactly that path. Decide between a cross-session published-revision sidecar in workspace/ (the entry's proposal — a mapped reopen can then read CLEAN) and an explicit accepted false-dirty, but do not ship the flip unexamined; D-save-4's asymmetry stands either way — a false-clean tells the user their edits are safe in project.arbc when they are not, and is the unsafe direction. TEST IMPACT: most of tests/project_open_test.cpp's pinned cases invert. 'a workspace-mapped reopen restores the record graph but binds NO content' (:332) pins the retired premise and must be REWRITTEN to pin reconstruction, not deleted; ':407 rebuilds a content-bearing workspace', ':435 keeps the map for a content-free workspace' and ':461 a never-saved content-bearing project keeps the map and REPORTS the loss' all change meaning. Each inversion must be justified against the v0.4.0 changelog — these tests are the record of WHY the guard existed, and a silent rewrite loses it. SEE ALSO, not in scope: tasks/parking-lot.md 'Worker-backed parallel tile decode/encode' asks whether the WorkerPool's lifetime moves below render (an A4/A5 amendment). `open_document` takes the same LoadContext that carries the raster tile store, so a reconstructing reopen still decodes tiles INLINE — the cold-open cost moves from rebuild to map-plus-reconstruct rather than disappearing. This leaf ships correctly without answering that, but it is the cheapest moment to answer it, since it already owns an architecture amendment on this path. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc workspace-map reopen binds no Content' + 'Cross-session dirty precision on mapped-workspace reopen'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A13/A19, docs/00-design.md D16, D-open-3."
    }
  }

  # ---- Canvas & rendering (A4/A5/A6) ----
  task canvas "Canvas & rendering" {
    task view "Canvas view = HostViewport + InteractiveRenderer; tile -> GL" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.project.app_state
      note "A canvas view drives a libarbc HostViewport + InteractiveRenderer over the shared Document and uploads settled CPU tiles as GL textures. A GPU Backend later needs no editor change (behind the Backend seam). Arch A4/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/canvas_view.md"
    }
    task frame_sync "UI-thread <-> driver frame handoff; edit submission" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !view
      note "The driver renders off the UI thread; the UI displays the latest frame (double-buffer) and SUBMITS edits, never touching the cache. Honors the library's single-writer / leaf-only-dispatch contract. Arch A4. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/frame_sync.md"
    }
    task multi_canvas "N canvases over one Document sharing one WorkerPool" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !frame_sync
      note "Multiple canvas views = multiple HostViewport/InteractiveRenderer over the one Document sharing one library WorkerPool; each cache render-thread-confined, so no new locking. This is 'paint through Viewport | look through Hero'. Arch A5 / D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/multi_canvas.md"
    }
    task nav "Pan / zoom the viewport camera; scale bar; deep zoom" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !view
      note "Pan/zoom drives the active viewport camera (transient); a scale bar in composition units (no phantom %); deep-zoom rebasing surfaced from HostViewport. Design: D2. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/nav.md"
    }
    task tool_dispatch "Route ToolSelection into canvas pointer-gesture dispatch" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.dock.tool_rail, editor.cells.gizmo, editor.canvas.nav
      note "Route dockmodel::ToolSelection active tool into the canvas interaction handler so a canvas pointer gesture dispatches per active tool (Select/Brush/Eyedropper/Pan); promote the tool->behavior seam into interact (hit-test/gizmo/brush math). Source of debt: tasks/refinements/editor/tool_rail.md (D20/A11). Design: docs/00-design.md D20, docs/01-architecture.md A11."
    }
    task fit_bounds "Wire reset-to-fit to root composition authored bounds" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !nav
      note "reset-to-fit currently restores identity/fit framing (D-canvas_view-5); wire it to the root composition's authored bounds once a Document composition-bounds accessor is available. The general interact::fit(content,pane) math ships in editor.canvas.nav tested for this future wiring + downstream reuse. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/nav.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D2 §3. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/fit_bounds.md"
    }
    task nav_aids "Deep-zoom navigation aids: fit-to-frame / fit-to-cell / zoom-to-selection" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !fit_bounds, editor.cells.selection
      note "The richer deep-zoom orientation aids beyond reset-to-fit (editor.canvas.nav/fit_bounds), reusing interact::fit(content,pane): fit-to-frame (fit a chosen camera frame to the pane — cameras only), fit-to-cell and zoom-to-selection (frame the selected cell / current selection — need the selection model). Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19, resolves the D2 §3 open marker + parking-lot 'Deep-zoom navigation aids'): all three are in scope. The overview's in-panel viewport manipulation + zoom control is a SEPARATE surface owned by editor.panels.overview, not this leaf. Design: docs/00-design.md D2 §3. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/nav.md, tasks/refinements/editor/fit_bounds.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/nav_aids.md"
    }
    task edit_render_sync "Serialize writer-thread Document edits against CanvasHost render reads (TSAN)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !multi_canvas
      note "Pre-existing TSAN race: render thread's for_each_content (CanvasHost render_frame) reads arbc's writer-thread-owned d_contents while an off-thread damage()/add_content mutates it; no synchronization. Surfaced under container CPU contention in CI. Serialize writer-thread Document edits against the render-read path (arbc dep or CanvasHost edit/render contract). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/model.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/edit_render_sync.md"
    }
    task blank_first_frame "Don't advance published frame sequence until first composited non-empty tile" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !frame_sync
      note "CanvasDriver publishes a blank first frame and advances frames_issued for it, making any frame-count-based settle heuristic unsound under sanitizer/container slowdown (the settle fires on the blank frame). Don't advance the published sequence until a frame has composited non-empty tile content. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/model.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/blank_first_frame.md"
    }
    task nested_composition_binding "Wire KindBridge/Registry DocumentBinding in CanvasRenderer for nested composition rendering" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !blank_first_frame
      note "The interactive CanvasRenderer does not wire a KindBridge/Registry DocumentBinding, so a nested-composition canvas renders no coverage (blank) in production. Wire the DocumentBinding so nested cells render correctly in the interactive path. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/blank_first_frame.md (2026-07-19). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/nested_composition_binding.md"
    }
    task arbc_v020 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.2.0" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !nested_composition_binding, !edit_render_sync
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.1.0->v0.2.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25); build + all goldens green (v0.2.0 is additive — every 0.1.0 registration compiles unchanged). Activates the lock-free copy-on-write content-binding render read path (ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#10, closed by #11: content bindings publish COW behind an atomic shared_ptr<const ContentBindings>), so the render walk of d_contents no longer races the writer — making doc_mu's read guard redundant. Prereq for single_writer and cameras.reopen_slab_adopt. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4."
    }
    task single_writer "Retire doc_mu: render read lock-free via libarbc v0.2.0 COW content bindings" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v020
      note "arbc v0.2.0 (#10/#11) publishes the id->Content* bindings copy-on-write behind an atomic, so the render-thread for_each_content/resolve walk reads a stable snapshot while a UI-thread add_content rebinds — the d_contents read race edit_render_sync's doc_mu papered over is gone in the library. Retire doc_mu (declaration, the drive_once hold, the apply_edit wrap) and keep apply_edit as the writer-thread edit seam. Supersedes editor.canvas.edit_render_sync's D-edit_render_sync-1 (doc_mu now redundant on the read side). NOTE the residual: HostViewport::settle_external_loads is itself a render-thread writer-publish, a second writer *identity* (arbc#7) that doc_mu never fixed — inert on documents with no pending external loads (every path exercised today). It is a LIBRARY-owned contract violation — HostViewport::step() publishes the arrival on the render thread — filed upstream as ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#13; the host cannot fix it without a library change. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A4.1. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/single_writer.md"
    }
    task focused_canvas_indicator "Show which canvas the framing-derived verbs act on" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cameras.mint_from_focused_canvas
      note "editor.cameras.mint_from_focused_canvas makes window focus semantically load-bearing — the focused canvas decides what 'New Shot From View' promotes and where an inserted cell lands — but leaves it invisible: ImGui's own focus chrome is a title-bar tint that is easy to miss in a two-pane dock and is absent while the rail holds focus. Draw a passive marker on the pane CanvasView::focused_view_id() names (a one-pixel accent border inside the pane rect, or a small badge beside the existing camera-picker overlay at src/app/canvas_view.cpp:236-245), driven from the sticky hint rather than from live ImGui focus so it persists across the rail click — plus an e2e phase asserting the marker follows WindowFocus and survives a rail interaction. Nothing in dockmodel, nothing in dock (D18's 'no privileged editor area' is about layout, not about indicating where the pointer verb applies). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/mint_from_focused_canvas.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23, D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/focused_canvas_indicator.md"
    }
    task accent_palette "Hoist the canvas accent colour into a shared alpha-parameterized constant" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !focused_canvas_indicator
      note "IM_COL32(120, 200, 255, α) now appears at five sites in src/app/canvas_view.cpp with no shared definition and three distinct alphas (40, 220, 255; the amber at :296 is a different hue). A blanket rename cannot consolidate them — the alphas differ — so this needs a decomposed constexpr RGB triplet plus an accent(uint8_t alpha) helper applied at every site, with the existing goldens and e2e pixel probes proving byte-identical output. Prerequisite for any future theming. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/focused_canvas_indicator.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/accent_palette.md"
    }
    task view_id_natural_order "Order canvas view ids numerically, not lexicographically, in the framing fallback" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !focused_canvas_indicator
      note "framing_for_focus/focus_target's 'lowest-id sized pane' fallback consumes panes_by_id in the order std::map<std::string, Presenter, std::less<>> yields (lexicographic), so with ten or more canvases open 'canvas#10' sorts before 'canvas#2' and the fallback picks the wrong pane. Fix by ordering the projection in CanvasView::framing_for (src/app/canvas_view.cpp:555-567) with a natural/numeric-suffix comparator, keeping focus_target itself order-agnostic and pure; Catch2 cases covering canvas#2 vs canvas#10 ordering, plus an e2e that opens ten panes. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/focused_canvas_indicator.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23, D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/view_id_natural_order.md"
    }
    task arbc_v030 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.3.0" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !single_writer
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.2.0->v0.3.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25); build + all goldens green (v0.3.0 is additive — every 0.2.0 call compiles and behaves unchanged on a single-threaded host). Lands the two library-owned items this editor filed: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#13 (HostViewport::step() no longer publishes structural writes off the writer thread — it asks Model::on_writer_thread() and reports the owed install as StepOutcome::external_loads_ready) and its companion, the DamageAccumulator flush/drain race (now mutex-guarded for that handoff alone), which together retire A4.1a's writer-priority document lease justification; plus ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#15 (Journal cursor/depth published as atomics, so the UI's per-frame can_undo()/can_redo() at src/dock/dock.cpp:337,343 are any-thread reads). New surface consumed downstream: Document::on_writer_thread(), Document::external_loads_ready(), Document::set_external_load_settler()/external_loads_auto_settled(), HostViewport::Config::external_loads_ready. Prereq for writer_thread. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A4.1/A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v030.md"
    }
    task history_published_reads "Publish journal entry names for any-thread History-panel reads" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v030
      note "arbc::Journal::can_undo()/can_redo()/depth()/cursor() became atomics in v0.3.0 (#15), but entry_at() (arbc/model/journal.hpp:132) did not — it returns a reference into the writer-owned d_entries vector, excluded explicitly by #15. The History panel calls it three times per frame from the UI thread (src/views/views.cpp:187,190,211). Safe today only because the UI thread IS the writer — the exact property editor.canvas.writer_thread removes. Fix: have commands::AppState maintain a published shared_ptr<const std::vector<std::string>> of entry names, refreshed on the writer thread after each commit/navigate and loaded atomically by the panel, so views.cpp reads no journal internals at all. Catch2 coverage for the publish/refresh logic in L1 commands plus a TSan-lane concurrent-read case; existing tests/history_e2e_test.cpp must pass unmodified. Must land BEFORE editor.canvas.writer_thread — otherwise that leaf ships a known TSan race. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v030.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1, A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/history_published_reads.md"
    }
    task writer_thread "Dedicated writer thread with sync + async closure submission" {
      effort 4d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !arbc_v030, !history_published_reads
      note "libarbc doc 15 § Thread rules (15-memory-model.md:185-202) requires a consumer whose writes originate on two threads to funnel them to ONE dedicated writer thread by posting the work as a task, never to take turns under a mutex — a consumer mutex re-covers accesses, not identity, and the lock-free growth path (relaxed high_water, SlabDirectory::publish, the writer-thread checkpoint seal) is written against one mutator. The editor writes from two threads: the UI thread (every verb, undo/redo, project_open.cpp:107 load_document, save.cpp:126 capture_snapshot) and the render thread (HostViewport's ctor/dtor call the writer-thread-only set_external_load_settler, host_viewport.cpp:115,130, on every add/remove and every resize rebuild). Add an L1 'writer' component (ace::writer::WriterThread over the platform::Threads spawn seam, base+platform only, no libarbc) with one FIFO and two entries — submit_sync (result-carrying verbs; most call sites capture by reference) and submit (coalescing gestures, plus the render thread's deduped external-arrival wake on StepOutcome::external_loads_ready) — an idle hook that PROACTIVELY consumes the work step() deferred (on every queue drain: settle when Document::external_loads_ready() is non-zero, stay armed for a bounded re-poll only while pending_external_loads() is in flight, so an arrival on a fully idle app installs with no submission from anywhere and a document with no external refs costs zero wakeups) — an inline degenerate mode that doubles as the headless-fixture and WASM/A3 fallback, and drain-then-join teardown enclosing the document's lifetime. Retires A4.1a's writer-priority lease (doc_mu/doc_cv/doc_busy/doc_waiting_writers/Impl::Lease, the run() hold, CanvasHost::apply_edit) and moves the edit seam out of L2 render; migrates the per-renderer render-confined KindBridge (canvas_renderer.cpp:40,140-142) to one document-scoped writer-owned bridge, since v0.3.0 runs the settle on the writer and the document holds one settler slot. Tripwire: doc.on_writer_thread() true inside every posted closure, false on the UI and render threads. Promotes tasks/parking-lot.md 'Async submit-queue (off-UI-thread writer)' with its framing corrected — this is a memory-model requirement, not a profiling-gated latency optimization. Scope is the WHOLE migration — a partial one is not a shippable state (a writer thread plus any remaining UI-thread write = two identities, worse than today): the verbs are a rebind of the existing run_edit/apply_edit funnel (shell.cpp:283, project_gateway.cpp:106-343, camera_inspector.cpp:92,118, canvas_view.cpp:403), but project open/create (project_open.cpp:107 load_document, which BINDS the identity, reached from commands/app_state.cpp:93,99 and the shell.cpp:58 bootstrap) and save (save.cpp:128 capture_snapshot + checkpoint) are not on that seam and carry the lifetime restructuring. Only the sync->async tuning of individual gestures is deferrable, under measurement. Supersedes editor.canvas.single_writer's run-on-the-caller seam. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4/A4.1/A4.1a. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/writer_thread.md"
    }
    task pending_removes_order "A CanvasHost remove pre-empts a still-queued add for the same id" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !writer_thread
      note "CanvasHost::drive_once services removes with a bulk clear (src/render/canvas_host.cpp:319-329) while it consumes pending_resizes/pending_cameras PER-ID — erasing only the requests it actually applied, because step 3's own rule is that a request for an id that is not live YET stays QUEUED. A remove landing after the iteration's pending_adds.swap (:288-291) but before the entry-map lock names exactly such an id: the bulk clear drops it, and the queued add then resurrects the entry the caller removed. DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-24): a remove PRE-EMPTS a still-queued add — the entry never surfaces. So consume pending_removes per-id like the other two maps, and have a remove for a not-yet-live id cancel that id's queued add (and its queued resize/camera) instead of being dropped; a remove naming an id that is neither live nor queued stays a no-op, not an error, matching add's existing idempotence (:294-296). Catch2 coverage in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp for add-then-remove within one iteration, a remove interleaved between the swap and the lock, and a remove for an unknown id; the existing TSan anchor must pass unmodified. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'CanvasHost pending_removes drop window' (D-canvas_host-pending_removes-drop, fixer note during editor.canvas.accent_palette). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/pending_removes_order.md"
    }
    task arbc_v040 "Bump libarbc pin to v0.4.0" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !writer_thread
      note "Bump ARBC_GIT_TAG v0.3.0->v0.4.0 (CMakeLists.txt:25); the tag landed on ruoso/arbitrarycomposer 2026-07-28, so this leaf is unblocked on the library side. UNLIKE v0.2.0 and v0.3.0 this pin is NOT purely additive — three consumer-visible changes land with it and this leaf OWNS absorbing them. (1) ONE RENDER PATH: the untiled `arbc::render_frame` free function is REMOVED and `render_offline` renders through the same tiled driver the interactive loop uses, separated only by `Exactness`. The editor never called `render_frame` (src/render/render.cpp:30,52 call `render_offline` only), so nothing fails to compile — but a still export composites slightly differently where the two paths disagreed: the retired driver sized its temp to exactly the content, so a minification's outer resampling tap fell on that surface's transparent border and rang the value up. (2) TILE APRON: every tile surface grows 256^2 -> 264^2 device px and paints only its own cell through the new `Backend::composite_windowed`, and a finite `bounds()` is now enforced in the tile's own pixels instead of `compositor.bounded_content_tile_clip`'s device-space round-out clip (retired upstream). The extent boundary therefore antialiases through the composite's own tap rather than clipping to whole device pixels, and the previous up-to-1px of un-attenuated bleed is gone; separately an opaque fill is opaque again across tile boundaries at a fractional composite phase (it was alpha 0.75 every 256 device px on any sub-pixel pan, off-rung scale or rotation — invisible to every landed golden because they all composite at an integral phase). (3) `arbc::Backend` gains `composite_windowed` and `clear_rect` as virtuals a pixel-modelling implementation must supply; the editor implements no `Backend` and has no such double (render.cpp:26,50, canvas_renderer.cpp:183 and tests/binding_test.cpp:9 all use `arbc::CpuBackend`), so this is inert here — it is the one change that would break a future editor-side backend. `SurfaceRef` also gained an optional `origin`; the editor provides no surfaces. EXPECTED TEST CHURN, owned here: re-baseline tests/goldens/export_camera_64x64.png and any byte-exact composite assertion, and JUSTIFY each diff against the v0.4.0 changelog rather than accepting it — a golden that moves for a reason the changelog does not name is a regression, not a re-baseline. Also retarget tests/arbc_pin_test.cpp at v0.4.0: its existing statements stay true (`Document::open` survives unchanged and is still record-only) and it should additionally pin the guarantees the consumer leaves below lean on. THREE PARKED ITEMS CLEAR WITH THE PIN: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#17 (a deferred-external nested child no longer composites blank under the real WorkerPool) and #18 (a magnified raster leaf renders at the requested scale under BestEffort, so `schedule_follow_up` stops being true forever and the render thread stops burning a core) each get an assertion leaf below; #26 (removed content is reclaimed at the last release once its removal leaves history) needs NO editor change — the insert/delete memory growth is simply gone. ONE CONTRACT CHANGE WITH NO EDITOR WORK: #23 states `Content::bounds()` ANY THREAD and puts a kind with a mutable extent on notice to publish the change atomically; `scene::CameraContent::bounds()` is constant-empty (src/scene/ace/scene/camera.hpp:46-48) and every other kind the editor uses is a library built-in, so `pick_targets`' UI-thread read is now contract-backed and the TSan case in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp stops being a tripwire and becomes an assertion of a stated guarantee. NEW SURFACE CONSUMED DOWNSTREAM, each by its own leaf: `arbc::open_document`/`ReopenedDocument::unreconstructed`/`Document::rebind_content`/`recovered_content_state()`/`set_content_identity_capture` (#19, editor.project.reconstructing_reopen); `Document::create_content_and_attach`/`remove_contents` (#20, editor.cells.one_action_one_entry); `Registry::insert_schema`/`arbc::KindInsertSchema` and org.arbc.solid's new `r,g,b,a,x,y,w,h` grammar (#21/#22, editor.cells.insert_schema); `Journal::history()` (#24, editor.canvas.history_snapshot_adopt); `HostViewport::attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()`/`Config::install_settler` (#25, editor.canvas.settler_attach_split); `render_offline(document, pinned, viewport, backend)` (#27, editor.cameras.export_pinned). Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md triage 2026-07-28. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A1/A5/A6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/arbc_v040.md"
    }
    task nested_real_pool "Assert the deferred-external nested child composites under the real WorkerPool" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "tests/canvas_host_test.cpp:1622-1628 DOCUMENTS but does not assert the gap: under the real interactive WorkerPool a deferred-external nested child composited BLANK even pre-settled, while the inline degenerate WriterThread composited it byte-exact — and the comment defers the composite assertion to the inline cases, naming `editor.canvas.nested_real_pool` as the follow-up that would own it. That follow-up is this leaf: ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#17 shipped in v0.4.0 (runtime.interactive routes a deferred EXTERNAL nested child's arrival), so the real-pool case can now assert the composite directly. Assert it there, and DELETE the NOTE that says this leaf does not own it — a stale disclaimer pointing at a closed gap is worse than none. The surrounding install-counting assertions (D-writer_thread-10's three idempotent paths) are unaffected and must pass unmodified. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'Deferred-external nested child composites blank under real WorkerPool'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5."
    }
    task magnified_raster_idle "Assert a magnified raster canvas reaches idle" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#18 shipped in v0.4.0 (kinds render at the requested scale under BestEffort), so a ~10x magnified 32x32 org.arbc.raster cell no longer reports `schedule_follow_up` true on every step forever — the defect that made the render thread burn a core for as long as the framing held and violated 02-architecture#idle-viewport-issues-no-frames. Assert the fix: a case that frames a raster cell with Shift+F (editor.canvas.nav_aids) and REQUIREs the canvas reaches quiescence — `frames_issued` stops advancing with an empty in-flight tile queue — at magnification, mirroring the 1x raster and nested-solid fixtures in the same file that were already quiet. KEEP tests/canvas_nav_e2e_test.cpp:139's wall-clock-bounded `settle()` helper exactly as it is: it is lane protection against ANY non-idling canvas, not a workaround for this one bug, and unbounded it tripped the ImGui Test Engine's 60 s ConfigWatchdogKillTest and turned a 16 s ace_shell_test into a 566 s failure. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'A magnified raster cell never lets the canvas go idle'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A5/A6."
    }
    task history_snapshot_adopt "Read the library's published history snapshot; retire the host-side mirror" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#24 shipped `Journal::history()` in v0.4.0: an any-thread pinned immutable snapshot (the `Model::current()` shape) carrying exactly the projection a panel draws — entry name plus per-entry byte_cost — with unchanged rows shared by pointer, so a commit copies N pointers rather than N strings and an undo republishes without allocating a row. That is the library-side answer to the ask editor.canvas.history_published_reads answered host-side under A18. Retire `commands::HistoryPublisher` (src/commands/ace/commands/app_state.hpp:118-123,202-207, the `publish_history` free function at :293, and every writer-thread refresh call site) and have the History panel read `journal().history()` directly. TWO PROPERTIES MUST NOT REGRESS: src/views/views.cpp still reads no journal internals other than a published snapshot (the A18 property — `entry_at()` stays writer-thread-only upstream, deliberately, so a direct call there is still a race); and the multi-step click-to-jump result seam (app_state.hpp:261-281, D-history_published_reads-4) stays in L1 commands — it is a VERB, not a read, and is untouched by this. tests/history_e2e_test.cpp must pass unmodified; tests/history_publish_test.cpp is retargeted at the library snapshot or retires with the publisher it covers. Per-entry byte cost becomes available to the panel for the first time — DISPLAYING it is editor.panels.history's call, not this leaf's. DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-28): retire the mirror rather than keep two publishers of the same fact. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc::Journal entry_at()/byte_cost() — upstream any-thread publication'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A18, A4.1."
    }
    task settler_attach_split "Let the render thread own HostViewport lifetime via attach/detach_settler" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040, !pending_removes_order, !arbc_router_attach_split
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#25 shipped `HostViewport::attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()` plus `Config::install_settler = false` in v0.4.0 — precisely the seam D-writer_thread-8 asked for. Today the render thread posts the WHOLE HostViewport construction and destruction to the writer thread through submit_sync (src/render/canvas_renderer.cpp:73,150; src/render/canvas_host.cpp:116) for one line inside the ctor: `Document::set_external_load_settler` is writer-thread-only. That costs a synchronous round trip per canvas add, per remove and per resize rebuild, and gives up scoped ownership of the viewport. Construct with `install_settler = false` on the render thread, post ONLY the `attach_settler()`/`detach_settler()` pair, and the render thread owns the viewport lifetime again. Upstream leaves the install counting and the N-viewports-per-document rule unchanged — only WHEN they happen moves — so the proof obligation is the existing TSan anchors in tests/canvas_host_test.cpp passing unmodified, plus the tripwire that `doc.on_writer_thread()` holds inside the attach/detach closures and NOWHERE else (a construction that quietly stayed posted would pass every test while delivering none of the benefit). ORDERING: depends on editor.canvas.pending_removes_order deliberately, not just on the pin. Both leaves rewrite `CanvasHost` — pending_removes_order changes how drive_once CONSUMES the pending maps (src/render/canvas_host.cpp:288-329), this leaf changes how the viewport entries those maps name are CONSTRUCTED and destroyed. Landing the 0.5d one first means it is written against the host as it stands today rather than rebased onto a restructured lifetime, and it keeps the add/remove pre-emption rule settled before the construction path moves underneath it. The reverse order costs a rebase of the smaller leaf for no gain. RE-DEFER 2026-07-28: arbc#25 split only the settler slot; DamageRouter::register_sink remains welded to the ctor — render-thread construction races the router. Blocked on arbc_router_attach_split consuming a future libarbc pin that moves the router registration into attach/detach. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'HostViewport settler attach/detach split'; tasks/refinements/editor/writer_thread.md D-writer_thread-8. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1/A5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/canvas/settler_attach_split.md"
    }
    task arbc_router_split_pin "Bump libarbc pin to the release that splits DamageRouter registration into attach/detach_settler" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_v040
      note "Placeholder for the next libarbc pin that moves DamageRouter::register_sink (and Document::set_damage_sink) out of HostViewport ctor/dtor into the attach_settler/detach_settler pair (or a dedicated attach_router/detach_router pair), gated by an install_settler-style flag. arbc#25 (v0.4.0) delivered only the settler-slot split; a successor issue must be filed upstream and merged before this pin task can land. When the upstream release ships: bump ARBC_GIT_TAG, confirm all goldens and TSan lanes green, and add complete 100. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/settler_attach_split.md (re-defer 2026-07-28 — arbc#25 split only the settler slot, not the router registrant). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1/A5."
    }
    task arbc_router_attach_split "Implement render-thread HostViewport construction once DamageRouter split ships in libarbc" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends !arbc_router_split_pin
      note "Once arbc_router_split_pin lands, consume the new API: construct HostViewport on the render thread with both the settler slot and the DamageRouter registrant deferred (install_settler=false + the router-split flag), post the now-complete attach pair (settler + router) via attach_settler/detach_settler (or the dedicated pair). This satisfies settler_attach_split's Constraint 3 — the single posted closure carries every writer-thread-only mutation — so settler_attach_split unblocks. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/canvas/settler_attach_split.md (re-defer 2026-07-28). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A4.1/A5."
    }
  }

  # ---- Cameras (D2/D9/D14) ----
  task cameras "Cameras" {
    task model "Viewport + shot cameras in the document; create" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.view
      note "Cameras are libarbc Viewports persisted as scene objects: the active viewport camera (screen-sized) plus saved shot cameras (own export resolution). Create / name / 'new shot from view'. Design: D2. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/model.md"
    }
    task rename_stable_id "In-place camera rename preserving ObjectId (Editable facet)" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model
      note "rename_camera currently replaces the Content+Layer (new ObjectId), breaking future selection/manip identity for cells.selection/cameras.manip; implement in-place content-state rename via the Editable facet to preserve ObjectId across renames. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/model.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/rename_stable_id.md"
    }
    task reopen_codec "Thread camera-kind registration into open_project (no PlaceholderContent on reopen)" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model
      note "scene::register_camera_kind is not threaded into project::open_project's rebuild-from-canonical registry; a project reopened from project.arbc degrades cameras to PlaceholderContent. Wire via a registration callback so open_project restores camera kinds with no project->scene edge. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/model.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/reopen_codec.md"
    }
    task workspace_reopen_slab "Fast-path reopen slab hook for editor-defined editable kinds" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !reopen_codec
      note "A checkpointed workspace containing an editor-defined editable Content (camera) aborts/corrupts on the Document::open fast-path remap (missing libarbc per-kind state-slab walk hook, model.cpp:771); a saved camera project crashes on fast-path reopen. Needs a libarbc bump exposing the slab hook, or an editor-side policy to force rebuild-from-canonical when custom editable kinds may be present. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/reopen_codec.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/workspace_reopen_slab.md"
    }
    task reopen_slab_adopt "Adopt libarbc v0.2.0 state-slab walk hook; retire A15 rebuild-from-canonical" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v020, !workspace_reopen_slab
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#5 shipped in v0.2.0: Registry::KindStateWalker (registered with the factory, looked up lock-free via Registry::state_walker) + Model::recovered_content_state() (the fast-reopen walk collects each reachable non-inert content StateHandle the model cannot descend) + arbc/runtime/recovered_state_replay.hpp's replay_recovered_content_state(). Register a camera-kind KindStateWalker, call replay_recovered_content_state on the Document::open map path, and retire A15's force-rebuild-from-canonical policy (reverses D-slab-2's durability cost). Also closes the never-saved-camera residual (D-slab-3): the fast path carries the camera's persisted state directly, no canonical floor needed. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/workspace_reopen_slab.md. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/reopen_slab_adopt.md"
    }
    task manip "Frame resize (hold resolution), aspect-lock, dutch, resolution inspector" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model
      note "Resize = re-crop (aspect-locked to resolution, holds resolution); resolution (pixel count + aspect) edited separately; dutch rotation modifier-gated. Design: D9. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/manip.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): dutch rotation is gated behind the SAME modifier chosen for cell shear (keep the two advanced rotate/shear gestures consistent; must not collide with Shift=constrain / Alt=from-center / Cmd-Ctrl=select-behind-bypass-snap / Space=pan). Resolution inspector ships aspect presets 16:9, 4:3, 1:1, 3:2 + a swap-orientation toggle (list is low-stakes, easily extended)."
    }
    task look_through "Activate a shot; multi-canvas look-through" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model, editor.canvas.multi_canvas
      note "Make a shot the active camera of a canvas ('look through Hero'); a second canvas can look through a shot for a live export preview beside the editing Viewport. Design: D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/look_through.md"
    }
    task frame_selection "Mint a camera fit to the current selection's placed extent" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.cells.selection, !model
      note "Implement docs/00-design.md:262-263 'frame selection' command: compute the union of the selected targets' placed extents (arbc::Affine::map_rect over interact::PickTarget, a pure L1 helper beside pick), derive a frame + resolution from it, and dispatch scene::add_camera as one commands::Command, with a rail/inspector affordance and an e2e. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/selection.md. Design: docs/00-design.md:262-263 §6. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/frame_selection.md"
    }
    task new_shot_from_view "Rail action: promote the viewport's current framing into a saved shot" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !frame_selection
      note "Add a second Edit-section rail item 'New Shot From View###new_shot_from_view' plus a ProjectGateway virtual pair; L4 impl reads ViewFraming (src/app/ace/app/view_framing.hpp, bound at src/app/shell.cpp:288), calls the already-shipped interact::new_shot_from_view (src/interact/ace/interact/interact.hpp:109), and dispatches commands::add_camera_command (shipped by editor.cameras.frame_selection) — plus an e2e. Closes the camera_inspector.cpp:41 'No cameras — create a shot from the view.' lie. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/frame_selection.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23, :64-66. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/new_shot_from_view.md"
    }
    task mint_from_focused_canvas "Promote the focused canvas's framing, not canvas#1's" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !new_shot_from_view
      note "CanvasView::primary_framing() (src/app/canvas_view.cpp:523-534) always returns the lowest-id sized presenter, so with two canvases open 'New Shot From View' promotes canvas#1 even when the user works in canvas#2. Add focused-canvas view id to CanvasView (set from ImGui::IsWindowFocused at draw time, fallback to lowest-id), expose focused_framing(), bind the gateway's framing provider to it, move insert_cell's provisional placement to the same source, and add a multi_canvas e2e. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/cameras/new_shot_from_view.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23, D18. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/mint_from_focused_canvas.md"
    }
    task export "Export via cameras (render_offline); PNG; batch" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model
      note "A camera IS the export spec: pick camera(s) -> library render_offline each at its resolution -> file(s). PNG (working->sRGB8); batch (one file per camera) + contact sheet fall out; transparent/filled bg, N-times scale multiplier; async with progress. Design: D14. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cameras/export.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): default background TRANSPARENT (preserves alpha; filled bg is a deliberate opt-in); N× multiplier default 1×; on filename collision OVERWRITE in place (batch export is idempotent, mirroring Save's re-dump — no auto-suffix, no prompt); contact sheet = uniform grid, each camera rendered then scaled to fit a common tile box preserving aspect, ceil(sqrt(N)) columns, thin gutters, a small per-tile camera-name caption, on the chosen background. NOTE: render_offline binds nested-composition operators as of libarbc v0.2.0 (ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#6, fixed) — nested children export correctly with the v0.2.0 pin (editor.canvas.arbc_v020)."
    }
    task contact_sheet "Contact sheet: all cameras tiled into one PNG" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !export
      note "Tile the same ExportPlan into one additional PNG: uniform grid, ceil(sqrt(N)) columns, each tile scaled to fit a common tile box preserving aspect (linear-working-space resample via arbc/media/image_resampler.hpp in L2 render, not an sRGB8 box filter), thin gutters, a small per-tile camera-name caption via an embedded fixed-width bitmap glyph table (ImGui font is L3-only, unavailable to a headless job). Chosen background matches the Export panel bg option. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cameras/export.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D14, :362; docs/01-architecture.md A20. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md"
    }
    task caption_latin1 "Contact-sheet captions: Latin-1 camera names" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !contact_sheet
      note "Decode the camera name from UTF-8 to code points inside commands, extend the embedded glyph table with the printable Latin-1 range (U+00A0–U+00FF), drive text_width/fit_text/draw_text off code points instead of bytes, and keep the fallback box for anything outside the table. Motivation: camera names are free text with no charset constraint (editor.cameras.rename_stable_id), and D3/D23's arbitrariness principle argues against silently boxing a name the user typed — 'Café' rendering as 'Caf□' is a visible defect. Tests: table-driven Catch2 cases over accented names and malformed UTF-8, plus one golden with an accented caption. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D23. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/caption_latin1.md"
    }
    task export_destination_reseed "Re-seed Export destination when service is pointed at a new project" {
      effort 0.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !contact_sheet
      note "The Export panel seeds its destination path (exports/ under the project directory) once when the ExportService is first created; on project reopen the panel retains the previous project's destination because the panel keyed its reset on the service pointer address, not a stable identity. Re-seed the destination whenever the service instance id changes, so the path always reflects the live project. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/contact_sheet.md (fixer follow-up). Design: docs/01-architecture.md A20. Refinement: tasks/refinements/cameras/export_destination_reseed.md"
    }
    task export_pinned "Pin the document once per batch export so every item renders one state" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !export_destination_reseed
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#27 shipped `render_offline(document, pinned, viewport, backend)` in v0.4.0 (arbc/runtime/offline.hpp:42) beside the pin-per-call overload. The pin-per-call shape is why an N-frame batch export pinned N times, so an edit landing mid-batch could make item 3 reflect a document state item 1 did not. D-export-8 chose to RECORD and REPORT that incoherence rather than block it (`ExportReport::document_changed_during_export`, src/commands/ace/commands/export.hpp:193, computed at export.cpp:377 as the end revision differing from the start revision, surfaced at src/views/views.cpp:463) because the host-side alternatives were untenable: blocking the writer for a whole batch contradicts D14's async promise, and snapshotting meant a full parse-and-rebuild per export. Take the pin ONCE for the batch and pass it to every item. A `DocStatePtr` retains its version, so there is no 'that revision is gone' error case a revision number would have needed. THE REPORTING FIELD THEN CHANGES MEANING and must not be left as-is: with one pin, the batch IS coherent, so `document_changed_during_export` stops being a coherence warning about the OUTPUT and becomes at most an informational note that the document moved on while the export ran. Either retire it with its views.cpp notice, or keep it and reword the notice to say what is now true — a stale warning telling the user their coherent export might be incoherent is worse than none. Cancellation stays honest at ITEM granularity (Constraint 10, export.cpp:250) — unchanged; a pin held across a cancelled batch just releases earlier. tests/export_test.cpp's revision cases are where the new invariant is pinned: an edit committed mid-batch must leave every item byte-identical to a batch with no concurrent edit. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'render_offline version-addressed offline render'. Design: docs/00-design.md D14, docs/01-architecture.md A20."
    }
    task truetype_captions "Bundled TrueType face for offline text; retire the bitmap glyph table" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      depends !caption_latin1, editor.canvas.arbc_v040
      note "DECIDED (parking-lot triage 2026-07-28): ship a real bundled font and retire the embedded bitmap glyph table. editor.cameras.contact_sheet chose an embedded 5x7 constexpr ASCII table (D-sheet-4) as the defensible interim and editor.cameras.caption_latin1 extended it to the Latin-1 printable range; this leaf replaces both. THE FACE AND ITS LICENCE (decided 2026-07-28, human sign-off): bundle INTER REGULAR under the SIL OPEN FONT LICENCE 1.1, filling the `assets/ icons, fonts (bundled)` slot docs/01-architecture.md:247 has reserved and nothing has ever filled. OFL 1.1 explicitly permits bundling and redistribution inside software, commercially included, and Inter carries NO RESERVED FONT NAME — which is the load-bearing property here, because OFL's RFN clause forces a rename on any MODIFICATION and subsetting the face to the Latin-1 range this leaf needs IS a modification. Inter is additionally designed for small-size legibility and to work UNHINTED, which is exactly the regime stb_truetype imposes (it ignores hinting entirely, so a face that leans on strong hinting — DejaVu Sans, say — surrenders its advantage here). TRAP: Inter ships as a VARIABLE font by default and stb_truetype does not support variable axes. Vendor the STATIC `Inter-Regular.ttf` with TrueType `glyf` outlines, which is stb's best-tested path — never `InterVariable.ttf`, never a CFF/OTF build. ALTERNATIVE, if a more conservative face is ever wanted: DejaVu Sans (Bitstream Vera plus Arev licence, equally permissive, ubiquitous on the Linux v1 target) — that is a one-line change to this decision, not a redesign. THIS LEAF ESTABLISHES THE REPO'S FIRST OUT-OF-BAND LICENCE RECORD: third-party licences are recorded NOWHERE today. `third_party/stb/stb_image_write.h` carries its dual MIT/public-domain text INLINE in the header, which works for source and cannot work for a binary `.ttf`, and there is no root LICENSE and no third_party/LICENSE. So ship `OFL.txt` beside the font binary and state the vendored set (the font plus stb_truetype) somewhere editor.packaging.package will carry INTO the installed artifact. A bundled font whose licence text does not travel with it is the actual legal exposure, and it is created at packaging time, not at vendoring time. EMBED, DO NOT LOAD AT RUNTIME: the 5x7 table's quiet virtue was that it was constexpr — no file I/O, no runtime asset path, nothing for a headless commands-level job to fail to find and no FILE* route around platform::FileSystem. Preserve that by turning the `.ttf` into a generated constexpr byte array at BUILD time (a CMake step), so the face is linked in exactly as the table was. A runtime font path would make caption rendering depend on installed-artifact layout, which is a new failure mode for the offline job and a regression against D-sheet-4's reasoning. THE DEPENDENCY IS THE EASY PART: `third_party/stb/stb_truetype.h` goes beside the already-vendored `stb_image_write.h` under the identical containment idiom — instantiated by exactly ONE TU, a PRIVATE include dir on ace_commands (CMakeLists.txt:192-198), and a new scripts/check_levels.py EXTERNAL_ALLOWED entry confining it to {'commands'} exactly as `stb_write` is confined today. THE REAL BLOCKER IS THE PIPELINE, not the header: D-sheet-2's copy-only invariant. stb_truetype rasterizes an 8-bit COVERAGE bitmap, so a glyph edge is an ALPHA BLEND and not a copy of an opaque texel. src/commands/glyphs.cpp's text_width/fit_text/draw_text and the contact-sheet tile composite (src/commands/contact_sheet.cpp) must composite coverage over the chosen background in the LINEAR working space, never as an sRGB8 blend. AMEND D-sheet-2 to scope the copy-only invariant to the tile IMAGE path and admit compositing for text — that scoping is the whole reason the interim table existed. KEEP the white-plus-black-shadow legibility pair: antialiasing makes an arbitrary background easier to read against, not unnecessary. RETIRE the 5x7 table, its Latin-1 extension and its fallback box — a real face covers Latin-1 natively and reports its own .notdef, so the missing-glyph box becomes the font's business — but keep a case pinning that an unmapped code point still renders SOMETHING rather than nothing. TESTS: the contact-sheet goldens re-baseline because caption shapes change; this leaf depends on editor.canvas.arbc_v040 so the tile-apron re-baseline settles FIRST and the captions are re-baselined once instead of twice. caption_latin1's accented-name and malformed-UTF-8 cases are CHARSET cases, not glyph-table cases, and must survive the swap unmodified. Headless stays headless: ImGui's font atlas is L3-only and unreachable from a commands-level job, which is why the table existed; stb_truetype has the same property, so the offline path keeps working. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'Bundled font for offline text rendering' (DECIDED, item closed 2026-07-28). Design: docs/00-design.md D14, docs/01-architecture.md A20, :247."
    }
  }

  # ---- Cells & manipulation (D3/D7/D8) ----
  task cells "Cells & manipulation" {
    task model "Insert cell (image/raster/nested/solid) via Registry" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.canvas.view
      note "Insert a cell of any registered kind (org.arbc.image/raster/nested/solid) off the library Registry::ids(); place it in the composition by affine. Design: D3. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): the editor keeps NO kind allowlist — enumerate ALL Registry-advertised kinds uniformly (arbitrariness is the core principle); kind-specific content acquisition (raster resolution, image/nested file/URI, solid color) is the kind's concern, surfaced generically, not a hard-coded editor set. Cell resolution is a first-class user input at insert (NOT a fixed default — resolves the D3/00-design.md:116 open marker): the user specifies resolution, then PLACES the cell in the overview wireframe to see how it fits the composition. That placement gesture wants editor.panels.overview (not yet built) — this leaf ships the model-level insert (resolution + affine) with a provisional placement; the overview-wireframe placement lands with/after editor.panels.overview. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/model.md"
    }
    task selection "Project-level selection; hit-test; marquee; select-behind" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !model, editor.cameras.model
      note "One project-level selection (canvases are only cameras): click a cell BODY, a camera BORDER/label (interior click-through); marquee multi-select; Cmd/Ctrl-click select-behind. Shared across canvas/list/overview. Design: D7/D19. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/selection.md"
    }
    task gizmo "Transform gizmo: move / scale / rotate / shear; pivot; snapping" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends !selection
      note "Move (body), scale (corners proportional-by-default, edges 1D), rotate (Shift 15deg), shear (modifier), draggable pivot; snapping to cell edges/centers, camera frames, grid. Handle-drag is PLACEMENT, never resample. Design: D7/D8. Refinement: tasks/refinements/transform_gizmo.md"
    }
    task resolution "Cell resolution display; resample-to-crisp; health badge" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends !selection
      note "Show a cell's native/working resolution separately from placement; 'resample to crisp' grows a raster's grid; a referenced image is source-limited (the file is the floor). The health badge flags a camera out-resolving a cell. Design: D8. Refinement: tasks/refinements/resolution_ops.md"
    }
    task remove "Delete selected cell(s) via Document::remove_content + Delete chord" {
      effort 1d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends !selection
      note "Delete the selected cell(s) via arbc::Document::remove_content(content, composition, layer) (document.hpp:131 -- atomic, one journal entry, undoable) with the Delete chord and a rail/inspector affordance plus an e2e. Insert without delete is the gap editor.cells.model opens; the library verb already exists, so this is mechanical. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/model.md. Design: docs/00-design.md D7/D15. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor.cells/remove.md"
    }
    task insert_schema "Drive the insert dialog off the library's per-kind insert schema" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !model
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#21 shipped `arbc::KindInsertSchema` on the Registry in v0.4.0 — the fields a host must collect with their types, defaults, ranges and units, plus an `assemble` thunk turning collected strings into the kind's config, registered on the same atomic `Registry::add` as the factory (so a plugin cannot describe another kind's config after the fact) and read back through `Registry::insert_schema(id)`. The editor built its own `ace::scene::KindInsertSchema` + `InsertField` + per-kind grammar adapters in the meantime (src/scene/ace/scene/cell.hpp:37-99, src/scene/cell.cpp:207); that table is exactly what D-cells_model-2 predicted would shrink to zero once the API existed. Replace the editor's grammar adapters with the library's schema and its `assemble` thunk, so the grammar stays the KIND's own — the editor renders fields and hands back strings, and stops knowing that solid's separator is a comma. WHAT MUST NOT CHANGE: A16 / Constraint 2's no-allowlist property. `insert_schemas` still emits exactly one entry per `registry.ids()` entry, unconditionally and in registration order; a kind that registers NO schema upstream keeps the editor's raw-config fallback (`k_raw_config_field`, cell.hpp:66-72) and stays insertable end-to-end — that fallback is what makes the property testable and is NOT retired by this leaf. Also register a schema for the editor's OWN kind at src/scene/camera.cpp:441's `registry.add`, since the editor is now a plugin author by the same rule it applies to everyone else. RIDES ALONG — ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#22, org.arbc.solid's grammar now admits the extent the type always supported (`r,g,b,a,x,y,w,h` beside the unchanged `r,g,b,a`, origin plus size so a typo cannot express an inverted rect, non-positive size an error value): a Registry-built solid was always unbounded, so its layer transform was a NO-OP and placing, scaling or rotating one changed nothing on screen. That retires D-cells_model-3's accepted consequence and gives `scene::content_extent` a real answer for a factory-built solid instead of the honest `nullopt` (cell.hpp:100+), which is what `interact::place_in_view` needs BEFORE add_cell mints anything (Constraint 6). The built-ins default solid's extent upstream so an insert dialog produces a PLACEABLE solid. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc Registry per-kind insert-schema hook' + 'org.arbc.solid factory grammar admits no bounds field'. Design: docs/01-architecture.md A16, docs/00-design.md D3, :505-511."
    }
    task one_action_one_entry "One user-visible action is one journal entry for create and multi-delete" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.canvas.arbc_v040, !remove
      note "ruoso/arbitrarycomposer#20 shipped `Document::create_content_and_attach` (document.hpp:128) and `Document::remove_contents(std::span<const Removal>)` (:151) in v0.4.0; both publish once, append ONE entry, and reverse whole. Consume both. CREATE: `add_content` self-commits — it was the only vtable-binding call — so every cell and camera create costs two journal entries (content first, then transact -> add_layer -> attach_layer -> commit) and passes through a published state in which a content exists attached to nothing. src/scene/cell.cpp:305-308 says so in a comment and add_camera mirrors it (D-cells_model-7); both collapse to one call. DELETE: editor.cells.remove ships the correct single-object verb, but an N-object multi-select delete produces N entries and needs N undo presses to reverse something the user did ONCE (D-cells_remove-2); route it through `remove_contents` so a multi-select delete is one undo unit. The `(composition, layer)` naming and Constraint 5's leave-the-rest-untouched rule are unchanged — this changes journal GRANULARITY, not what is removed. PROOF OBLIGATION: assert entry COUNT, not just final state — the existing tests pass on both the two-entry and one-entry shapes, which is why the debt survived this long. Undo-once-restores-everything for an N-selection delete, and undo-once-removes-a-created-cell-whole, are the two cases that fail today. tests/cells_remove_test.cpp, tests/cells_remove_e2e_test.cpp and tests/cell_model_test.cpp carry them. Source-of-debt: tasks/parking-lot.md 'arbc Document lacks atomic create-content-and-attach' + 'arbc batch removal verb'. Design: docs/00-design.md D15, docs/01-architecture.md A17."
    }
  }

  # ---- Info panels (D5/D6/D10/D11) ----
  task panels "Info panels" {
    task inspector "Dense property sheet (transform / appearance / z-order / source)" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.cells.selection, editor.dock.view_registry
      note "A dense property sheet for the project selection: transform (units), appearance & stacking (opacity/blend/z-order), source (referenced/owned info + health). Design: D7 (density) / D11. Refinement: tasks/refinements/inspector.md"
    }
    task layers "Layers list (+ cameras); reorder; referenced-vs-painted; nested expand / enter" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends !inspector, editor.cells.model
      note "The layer list (cameras section + layers front->back); reorder = z-order; the borrowed/owned (referenced-vs-painted) distinction shown; a nested cell can be expanded (peek) or entered (isolation scope + breadcrumb). Design: D6/D11/D17. Refinement: tasks/refinements/layers.md"
    }
    task overview "Patterned-fill wireframe overview; z-order occlusion; editable" {
      effort 4d
      allocate team
      depends !layers, editor.cameras.model
      note "The render-free overview: each cell a hatch pattern (shared identity with the list), front fills occlude back with always-on-top dotted borders (z-order without a list), cameras as frames, the live viewport highlighted; editable (drag to place) + navigator + export/shot map. Design: D6/§5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/overview.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): hatch density is CONTENT-space (scales/tilts with the cell so scale/orientation read for free). The overview is a full viewport NAVIGATOR — manipulate the active viewport with an in-overview zoom control (partners D2 deep-zoom aids, editor.canvas.nav_aids), NOT navigation-only — and it is the placement surface for editor.cells.model inserts (a new cell is placed here). Remaining open items are visual polish only (hatch style/opacity, pattern-count-before-color, camera visual language)."
    }
    task color "sRGB picker; eyedropper (through camera); linear boundary" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.dock.view_registry, editor.paint.brush
      note "A conventional sRGB/HSV/hex picker + straight-alpha opacity; the editor is the invisible translator to/from the library's premultiplied-linear working space. Eyedropper samples the displayed sRGB through the active camera. Design: D10. Refinement: tasks/refinements/color.md"
    }
    task history "History view/panel: journal entry list + click-to-jump" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      complete 100
      depends editor.project.undo, editor.dock.view_registry
      note "Render journal().entry_at(i).name as an ordered list with the cursor() position marked; 'Undo <name>'/'Redo <name>' labels; click-an-entry to jump (loop undo()/redo() to reach the target cursor — single-step navigation only). L3 views. Design: D15/D18/D21. Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/undo.md. Refinement: tasks/refinements/editor/history.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Painting (D4/D5/D12) ----
  task paint "Painting" {
    task brush "Brush tool; %-of-view size + ring; dab into org.arbc.raster" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends editor.cells.model
      note "The brush tool: size in % of view (shorter edge, log slider) with an on-canvas ring; strokes dab into an org.arbc.raster cell's editable facet, coalesced into one undo step. Design: D5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/brush.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): brush size is SCREEN-locked only — a canvas-locked (composition-units) mode is deliberately NOT offered; it contradicts the model's principle (brush is screen-relative % of view; resolution is cell-owned, detail floor at the cell). No size-lock toggle."
    }
    task paint_res "Cell-fixed resolution painting; screen->cell mapping; detail-floor readout" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends !brush
      note "Dabs rasterize into the CELL's fixed working grid; the brush footprint maps camera px -> composition units -> cell px by affine composition. The '~N px on <cell>' readout doubles as the resolution-health / detail-floor cue. Design: D4/D5. Refinement: tasks/refinements/paint_res.md"
    }
    task retouch_stack "Add-paint-layer-above a referenced photo (non-destructive)" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends !brush, editor.import.image
      note "Attempting to paint a read-only (referenced) photo offers a one-gesture 'add a retouch layer above' — a new owned raster over its bounds; the original is never edited in place. Design: D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/retouch_stack.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Import & assets (D11/D12/D13) ----
  task import "Import & assets" {
    task image "Drop / place an image -> borrowed cell (1:1 px->units)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.cells.model
      note "Drop or Place an image file -> a borrowed (referenced) read-only org.arbc.image cell at the drop point, sized native px -> composition units 1:1 so pixel dimensions carry real relative scale. Design: D11/D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/import_image.md"
    }
    task paste "Paste / clipboard bitmap -> owned image" {
      effort 1.5d
      allocate team
      depends !image
      note "A pasted bitmap has no source file to borrow, so the project MINTS it as an owned read-only image in assets/; otherwise it behaves like an import. Design: D11/D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/import_paste.md"
    }
    task nested "Place another .arbc -> nested composition cell" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends !image
      note "Place another project (.arbc) -> an org.arbc.nested cell referencing it (the library's external nested reference). Design: D12. Refinement: tasks/refinements/import_nested.md"
    }
    task consolidate "Consolidate project (borrowed -> owned + relativize URIs) + relink missing borrows" {
      effort 2.5d
      allocate team
      depends editor.import.paste, editor.import.nested
      note "Consolidate copies borrowed files into assets/ and rewrites their URIs to project-relative (a reversible scene transaction, D15), producing a self-contained portable bundle; relink surfaces a locate-file / reveal affordance for a moved borrow. Design: D11/D12/D13, docs/00-design.md §8 (Import & assets: Consolidate, missing/moved -> placeholder + relink), D15 (consolidate reversible -> a dispatched transaction). Source-of-debt: tasks/refinements/editor/gc.md"
    }
  }

  # ---- Packaging & CI (native ship) ----
  task packaging "Packaging & CI" {
    task ci "Editor CI lanes (Linux / macOS / Windows)" {
      effort 2d
      allocate team
      depends editor.foundation.build
      note "Per-platform CI building the editor and running a headless smoke (window init + one rendered frame), matching the library's compiler matrix. Refinement: tasks/refinements/ci.md. Decided (pre-exec 2026-07-19): v1 is LINUX-ONLY — the full build + headless offscreen-GL smoke reusing the existing llvmpipe/act lane (docs/01-architecture.md:210-245). macOS and Windows lanes (their offscreen-GL rendered-frame smoke needs ANGLE/other, no llvmpipe) are DEFERRED to editor.packaging.package, which owns the release matrix. Title says tri-platform but scope here is Linux; retitle/rescope when packaging picks up mac/Win."
    }
    task package "Installable editor artifacts per platform" {
      effort 3d
      allocate team
      depends editor.panels.overview, editor.panels.color, editor.cameras.export, editor.paint.paint_res, editor.paint.retouch_stack, editor.import.paste, editor.import.nested, editor.cells.gizmo, editor.cells.resolution, editor.canvas.nav, editor.canvas.tool_dispatch, editor.cameras.manip, editor.cameras.look_through, editor.project.exec_new, editor.project.undo, editor.project.gc, editor.dock.tool_rail, editor.dock.workspaces
      note "Bundle the editor as an installable artifact per platform (AppImage/.dmg/.msi or CPack equivalents), embedding fonts/icons and staging the runtime. Gathers the feature-complete editor. Refinement: tasks/refinements/package.md"
    }
  }
}
